Configuration
*************

.[perex]
Where links and images point, what the HTML output looks like, which typography applies, what visitors may write in comments, and which Markdown and Texy syntaxes are enabled. Here you'll find every setting and its default value.

Everything is set through public properties of objects. For the blog from the [introduction |@home], you prepare them once, in one place, and then convert every article with them:

```php
$texy = new Texy\Texy;
$texy->config->headingTop = 2;
$texy->htmlOutput->imageBaseUrl = '/images/articles/';

$markdown = Texy\Markdown\Markdown::gfm(
	config: $texy->config,
	htmlOutput: $texy->htmlOutput,
	htmlPolicy: $texy->htmlPolicy,
);
$html = $markdown->toHtml($text);  // $text is an article written in Markdown
```

The settings are split into several objects, according to what they affect:

- `$texy->config` decides what the text means: typography, hyphenation, headings, and for Texy syntax also how its syntaxes are read
- `$texy->htmlOutput` decides what the resulting HTML looks like: image and link URLs, tags, classes and indentation
- `$texy->htmlPolicy` and `$texy->urlPolicy` guard what is allowed: HTML tags, classes, styles and URLs
- `$texy->allowedSyntax` turns individual syntaxes on and off

Markdown has the same objects. When you pass it the objects from `$texy` while creating it, as in the example above, both languages share them: you write a setting once and it applies to articles in Markdown and in Texy syntax alike, including any later change. Only `allowedSyntax` is separate for each language, because each has different syntaxes. The objects themselves cannot be replaced (`$texy->config = …` throws an error); you only change their values.

That is why the page has three parts: the [engine settings |#Engine], which apply to both languages, [Markdown |#Markdown] and [Texy syntax |#Texy Syntax]. Coming from version 3? The page [Upgrading from Texy 3 |upgrading] shows where the old settings moved.


Engine
======

The settings in this part apply equally to Markdown and to Texy syntax, provided Markdown shares the engine objects as shown at the top of the page.


Typography
----------

The [introduction |@home#Typography] shows what typography looks like. The language of the text, which tells Texy which quotation marks to use, defaults to `cs` (Czech):

```php
$texy->config->locale = 'en';
```

Texy knows `bg`, `cs`, `de`, `el`, `en`, `es`, `fr`, `hu`, `it`, `ja`, `pl`, `pt`, `ro`, `ru`, `sk`, `sl`, `tr` and `uk`. An unknown language throws `Texy\InvalidArgumentException` during conversion. To add another language, put it in the `locales` array. Its `quotes` key holds four quotation marks: the opening and closing primary ones, then the opening and closing nested ones.

```php
$texy->config->locales['xx'] = ['quotes' => ["\u{00BB}", "\u{00AB}", "\u{203A}", "\u{2039}"]];
$texy->config->locale = 'xx';
```

In Czech, Slovak and Polish, a one-letter preposition or conjunction must not be left at the end of a line, so Texy joins it to the following word with a non-breaking space. In Czech these are `k`, `s`, `v`, `z`, `o`, `u` and `i`, lowercase and uppercase, plus an uppercase `A`. The words are listed in the `oneLetterWords` key of the language's entry in the `locales` array, and a language without that key gets no non-breaking spaces. You can change the list, or add one to another language:

```php
$texy->config->locales['cs']['oneLetterWords'] .= 'a';  // also bind the Czech conjunction a
```

Texy breaks long Czech and Slovak words by inserting soft hyphens, invisible characters where the browser may break the word. It splits words by Czech syllables, so it leaves text in any other language whole:

```php
$texy->config->hyphenationWordLimit = 20;  // hyphenate words of 20 characters or more
```

You can turn off both typography and hyphenation:

```php
$texy->config->typography = false;
$texy->config->hyphenation = false;
```


Headings
--------

A blog article sits below the page title, so its headings should start at `<h2>`. That is what `headingTop` takes care of:

```php
$texy->config->headingTop = 2;           // the top heading becomes <h2>, default 1
$texy->config->headingIds = true;        // every heading gets an id, default false
$texy->config->headingIdPrefix = 'toc-'; // prefix of the generated ids
```

The heading `# How We Dried Plums` then becomes `<h2 id="toc-how-we-dried-plums">` and `## Harvest` becomes `<h3 id="toc-harvest">`. A heading that would end up deeper than `<h6>` after the shift stays `<h6>`.

Texy syntax can also balance headings, which means it works out by itself which heading in the article is the most important. [Heading Balancing |#Heading Balancing] explains how.

To build a table of contents or the page's `<title>`, read the headings from the document. Instead of `toHtml()`, convert the text in two steps so you can get at the document:

```php
$document = $markdown->parse($text);
$markdown->transform($document);

foreach ($document->find(Texy\Nodes\HeadingNode::class) as $heading) {
	echo $heading->level, ' ', $heading->getTitle(), ' ', $heading->id, "\n";
}

$html = $markdown->renderHtml($document);
```

```
2 How We Dried Plums toc-how-we-dried-plums
3 Harvest toc-harvest
3 Drying toc-drying
```

`parse()` parses the text, `transform()` works out the heading levels and ids among other things, and `renderHtml()` creates the HTML from the document, the same HTML `toHtml()` would return. The page title is `$document->findFirst(Texy\Nodes\HeadingNode::class)?->getTitle()`. For Texy syntax, you call the same methods on `$texy`. What exactly happens in each step is described in [Architecture and Principles |architecture].


Image and Link Paths
--------------------

The author writes just `dryer.jpg` or `articles/harvest` in the article, and Texy puts a prefix in front of the relative URL:

```php
$texy->htmlOutput->imageBaseUrl = '/images/articles/';  // default null, no prefix
$texy->htmlOutput->linkBaseUrl = '/blog/';              // default null, no prefix
```

So `dryer.jpg` becomes `/images/articles/dryer.jpg` and a link to `articles/harvest` becomes `/blog/articles/harvest`. URLs that start with a slash or with a scheme such as `https:` or `mailto:`, and links to an anchor `#section`, stay exactly as the author wrote them.

When you tell Texy where the images are on disk, it finds out their dimensions and adds them to the HTML. The browser can then lay out the page before the images load:

```php
$texy->htmlOutput->imageBaseUrl = '/images/';
$texy->htmlOutput->imageBasePath = __DIR__ . '/www/images';
```

```html
<img src="/images/dryer.png" alt="Dryer" width="300" height="200">
```

Dimensions the author writes explicitly take precedence.


Links
-----

```php
$texy->htmlOutput->linkNoFollow = false;   // rel="nofollow" on absolute links
$texy->htmlOutput->obfuscateEmail = true;  // hide email addresses from bots
$texy->htmlOutput->shortenUrls = true;     // display long URLs shortened
```

With `linkNoFollow`, links to absolute URLs, that is, to other websites, get `rel="nofollow"`. Links within your site, such as `/contact`, do not.

`obfuscateEmail` inserts an HTML comment into the displayed address, so `jane@example.com` looks the same to the reader, but a simple bot harvesting addresses does not recognize it. `shortenUrls` shortens a long URL displayed as a link in the text to something like `https://example.com/…s/and/apples`. Both settings apply only to Texy syntax; Markdown displays URLs exactly as the author wrote them.


Images
------

An image with a caption below it is written in Texy syntax and turns into semantic HTML5. This one is also aligned to the right:

```texy
[* dryer.jpg .(Dryer in the attic) >] *** Photo: Jane Smith
```

```html
<figure style="float:right"><img src="dryer.jpg" alt="Dryer in the attic">
	<figcaption>Photo: Jane Smith</figcaption>
</figure>
```

By default, alignment to the left or right is an inline `float` style, both for a plain image and for an image with a caption. If your CSS already has classes for it, assign them:

```php
$texy->htmlOutput->floatClasses['left'] = 'left';
$texy->htmlOutput->floatClasses['right'] = 'right';
```

If your templates expect different HTML, change the tag and add a class as well. With a tag other than `figure`, the caption goes into a `<p>`:

```php
$texy->htmlOutput->figureTag = 'div';     // default 'figure'
$texy->htmlOutput->figureClass = 'figure'; // default null, no class
```

```html
<div class="figure right"><img src="dryer.jpg" alt="Dryer in the attic">
	<p>Photo: Jane Smith</p>
</div>
```

An image that stands alone on a line in Texy syntax gets the `figureTag` even without a caption. A paragraph with several images, and in Markdown even with a single one, is wrapped not in `<p>` but in `<div>`. You set a different tag in `imageParagraphTag`:

```php
$texy->htmlOutput->imageParagraphTag = 'p';  // default 'div'
```


Alignment
---------

Alignment modifiers (`.<`, `.>`, `.<>`, `.=`, and in table cells also the vertical `.^`, `.-`, `._`) as well as column alignment of Markdown tables produce an inline style by default, such as `style="text-align:center"`. If your CSS already has classes for it, assign them:

```php
$texy->htmlOutput->textAlignClasses['left'] = 'text-left';
$texy->htmlOutput->textAlignClasses['center'] = 'text-center';
$texy->htmlOutput->verticalAlignClasses['top'] = 'align-top';
```

The keys of `textAlignClasses` are `left`, `right`, `center` and `justify`; the keys of `verticalAlignClasses` are `top`, `middle` and `bottom`. An alignment without a class stays an inline style.


Element Tags and Classes
------------------------

```php
$g = $texy->htmlOutput;
$g->phraseTags['strong'] = 'b';           // bold text as <b> instead of <strong>
$g->horizontalRuleClasses['-'] = 'rule';  // class of a horizontal rule, by the character it is written with
$g->emoticonClass = null;                 // class of the <span> around emoticons
$g->passHtmlComments = true;              // keep HTML comments from the text in the output
```

`phraseTags` decides which tag each kind of emphasis gets. The keys name the meaning, not the syntax, so they apply to both languages: `strong` (bold), `emphasis` (italic, whether written as `*…*`, `//…//` or `_…_`), `inserted`, `deleted`, `superscript`, `subscript`, `span`, `abbreviation`, `quote`, and `code` for inline code. A horizontal rule written with hyphens `---` has the key `-`, one written with asterisks `***` the key `*`, in both languages. Underscores `___` in Markdown have the key `_`.


HTML Code Formatting
--------------------

Texy prints the HTML indented and wrapped so that it is easy to read:

```php
$g = $texy->htmlOutput;
$g->indent = true;       // indent nested blocks
$g->baseIndent = 0;      // starting indentation level, for HTML inserted into an indented template
$g->lineWrap = 80;       // wrap lines longer than 80 characters, 0 = no wrapping
$g->preserveSpaces = ['textarea', 'pre', 'script', 'code', 'samp', 'kbd'];  // tags inside which whitespace is left alone
```


Allowed HTML Tags, Classes and Styles
-------------------------------------

Three lists on `$texy->htmlPolicy` decide which parts of the HTML and of modifiers make it into the output. The value `true` allows everything, `false` nothing.

```php
$texy->htmlPolicy->allowedTags = [
	'b' => [],            // <b> without attributes
	'a' => ['href'],      // <a> with the href attribute only
	'img' => true,        // <img> with any attributes
];
```

The default list of tags contains the common HTML5 tags, each with all its attributes. The key `'*'` lists the attributes allowed on every tag. A tag missing from the list is displayed as text in the output, and an attribute that is not allowed disappears. This holds for HTML written in Texy syntax and in Markdown alike:

```texy
<b class="x">bold</b> <a href="https://example.com" onclick="x()">link</a> <i>italic</i>
```

```html
<p><b>bold</b> <a href="https://example.com">link</a>
&lt;i&gt;italic&lt;/i&gt;</p>
```

Tag and attribute names are compared case-insensitively, just as in HTML. A tag that HTML does not define, such as `<foo-bar>`, is displayed as text by default. It gets through only with `allowedTags = true`.

Classes and styles coming from Texy syntax modifiers are guarded by two more lists. IDs go into the class list with a leading hash:

```php
$texy->htmlPolicy->allowedClasses = ['photo', '#main'];  // default true
$texy->htmlPolicy->allowedStyles = ['color'];            // default true
```

Then `.[photo]` produces `class="photo"`, and `.[ad]` is dropped. The `class` and `style` attributes in HTML written directly into the text are not guarded by these lists; only `allowedTags` decides about them.

Texy 3 checked attributes against the HTML 4 definition and dropped every one that did not belong to the tag. Texy 4 lets through whatever `allowedTags` allows, because HTML5 has no fixed list of attributes and `data-*` or `aria-*` attributes would otherwise have to be listed one by one. If you want to restrict attributes, list them.


Allowed URLs
------------

`$texy->urlPolicy` restricts which URL schemes links and images may point to. The value is a regular expression; `null` means no restriction, which is the default:

```php
$texy->urlPolicy->linkPattern = '~https?:|ftp:|mailto:~A';
$texy->urlPolicy->imagePattern = '~https?:~A';
```

Only URLs with a scheme are checked. Relative URLs such as `articles/harvest` always pass. A link to a URL that is not allowed is not created, and only its text remains. When an image is rejected, Markdown keeps its description, while Texy syntax keeps the whole image markup as text.

`urlPolicy` belongs to `htmlPolicy`, which uses it to check URLs in HTML tags. So passing `htmlPolicy` to Markdown is enough, and the `urlPolicy` comes along with it. If you pass both, it must be exactly the `urlPolicy` that `htmlPolicy` uses; otherwise Markdown throws `Texy\InvalidArgumentException`.


Safe Mode
---------

Anyone can post comments under an article. Turning on safe mode takes a single call. For Markdown:

```php
$comments = Texy\Markdown\Markdown::gfm(config: $texy->config);
Texy\Markdown\Configurator::safeMode($comments);
$html = $comments->toHtml($comment);
```

For Texy syntax:

```php
$comments = new Texy\Texy;
Texy\Configurator::safeMode($comments);
$html = $comments->toHtml($comment);
```

Create a separate object for comments, not the one you use for articles. Safe mode changes the settings the language works with, and if comments shared them with articles, it would restrict the articles too. You can share only `config`, which safe mode does not touch. Comments in Markdown then get the same typography as articles.

Safe mode:

- forbids classes, IDs and inline styles from modifiers (sets `allowedClasses` and `allowedStyles` to `false`)
- allows only safe HTML tags: `a` with the `href` and `title` attributes, `abbr` with `title`, `b`, `br`, `cite`, `code`, `em`, `i`, `strong`, `sub`, `sup`, `q`, `small`, and the attributes `title`, `lang` and `dir` on all of them
- allows links only to `http:`, `https:`, `ftp:` and `mailto:`, images only to `http:` and `https:`
- adds `rel="nofollow"` to links to other websites
- in Markdown, turns off HTML comments in the output; in Texy syntax, turns off HTML comments, link definitions and images

So in a comment, both languages let `<b>` through as bold text and display `<script>` as text:

```
Great recipe! <script>alert(1)</script> <b>Thanks!</b>
```

```html
<p>Great recipe! &lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt; <b>Thanks!</b></p>
```

An image in a Markdown comment is displayed only when it points to `http:` or `https:`. An image with any other URL, such as `javascript:`, leaves only its description. In Texy syntax, images are turned off in safe mode, and if you want them in comments, turn them on with the `images` parameter. The same URL restriction applies to them, and image definitions stay turned off:

```php
Texy\Configurator::safeMode($comments, images: true);
```

For Texy syntax there are two more ready-made settings:

- `Texy\Configurator::disableImages($texy)` turns off all image syntaxes and removes `img`, `object`, `embed` and `applet` from the allowed tags
- `Texy\Configurator::disableLinks($texy)` turns off links, email addresses and link definitions, and removes the `a` tag


Markdown
========

`Texy\Markdown\Markdown` reads Markdown according to the CommonMark specification. The GitHub Flavored Markdown extensions (tables, task lists, strikethrough, URLs without angle brackets and the filter for dangerous tags) are turned on by a static factory:

```php
$markdown = Texy\Markdown\Markdown::gfm();   // CommonMark and all GFM extensions
$markdown = new Texy\Markdown\Markdown;      // CommonMark only
```

Both the constructor and `gfm()` accept the same settings objects: `config`, `htmlOutput`, `htmlPolicy` and `urlPolicy`. When you pass them from `$texy`, as shown [at the top of the page |#Configuration], everything in the [Engine |#Engine] part applies to Markdown. When you don't, Markdown creates its own with default values, except that it sets typography to English and turns hyphenation off. You then find the same objects at `$markdown->config`, `$markdown->htmlOutput` and so on:

```php
$markdown = Texy\Markdown\Markdown::gfm();
$markdown->config->locale = 'de';
```

You can also pass only some of the objects, for example only `config`, when Markdown should take over typography and headings but you want to set up the HTML differently.

Each syntax can be turned on or off separately in the `allowedSyntax` array, for example when you don't want tables or raw HTML in articles:

```php
use Texy\Markdown\Syntax;

$markdown->allowedSyntax[Syntax::Table] = false;
$markdown->allowedSyntax[Syntax::HtmlBlock] = false;
$markdown->allowedSyntax[Syntax::HtmlInline] = false;
```

|---
| Constant | Syntax
|---
| `Syntax::HeadingAtx` | heading `# Heading`
| `Syntax::HeadingSetext` | heading underlined with `===` or `---`
| `Syntax::BlockQuote` | block quote `>`
| `Syntax::List` | lists
| `Syntax::FencedCode` | code block between <code>&#96;&#96;&#96;</code> or `~~~`
| `Syntax::IndentedCode` | code block indented by four spaces
| `Syntax::ThematicBreak` | horizontal rule
| `Syntax::HtmlBlock` | block of raw HTML
| `Syntax::HtmlInline` | HTML tag in text
| `Syntax::LinkDefinition` | link definition `[name]: url`
| `Syntax::Link` | link `[text](url)`
| `Syntax::Image` | image `![description](url)`
| `Syntax::Delimiter` | emphasis `*italic*`, `**bold**` and the same with `_`
| `Syntax::Autolink` | URL in angle brackets
| `Syntax::AutolinkEmail` | email in angle brackets
| `Syntax::CodeSpan` | inline code
| `Syntax::Escape` | backslash before a special character
| `Syntax::Entity` | HTML entities such as `&amp;`
| `Syntax::HardBreak` | line break with two spaces
| `Syntax::EscapedBreak` | line break with a backslash
| `Syntax::SoftBreak` | ordinary line end inside a paragraph
| `Syntax::Table` | tables (GFM)
| `Syntax::TaskList` | task lists (GFM)
| `Syntax::Strikethrough` | strikethrough text `~~` (GFM)
| `Syntax::AutolinkExtended` | URLs without angle brackets (GFM)
| `Syntax::TagFilter` | defusing dangerous tags such as `<script>` (GFM)

Markdown has neither images with captions nor emoticons, so the `figureTag`, `figureClass` and `emoticonClass` settings do not affect it. An image standing alone in a paragraph is wrapped in the tag from `imageParagraphTag`, `<div>` by default.


Texy Syntax
===========


Which Syntaxes Are Enabled
--------------------------

The `$texy->allowedSyntax` array turns individual Texy syntaxes on and off. The names are also available as constants of the `Texy\Syntax` class:

```php
$texy->allowedSyntax['image'] = false;          // no images
$texy->allowedSyntax['phrase/ins'] = true;      // turn on ++inserted text++
$texy->allowedSyntax[Texy\Syntax::Emoticon] = true;
```

The array is read once at the start of a conversion; changing it during the conversion has no effect.

|---
| Key | Default | Syntax
|---
| `phrase/strong` | on | bold `**text**`
| `phrase/em` | on | italic `//text//`
| `phrase/em-alt` | on | italic `*text*`
| `phrase/strong+em` | on | bold italic `***text***`
| `phrase/code` | on | code <code>&#96;text&#96;</code>
| `phrase/notexy` | on | text without formatting `''text''`
| `phrase/span`, `phrase/span-alt` | on | `"text .[class]"` and `~text .[class]~`
| `phrase/quote` | on | inline quotation `>>text<<`
| `phrase/acronym`, `phrase/acronym-alt` | on | abbreviations `NBA((National Basketball Association))`
| `phrase/sup-alt`, `phrase/sub-alt` | on | superscripts and subscripts `x^2`, `O_2`
| `phrase/sup`, `phrase/sub` | off | superscripts and subscripts `x^^2^^`, `O__2__`
| `phrase/ins` | off | inserted text `++text++`
| `phrase/del` | off | deleted text `--text--`
| `phrase/quicklink` | on | link on a single word `word:[url]`
| `phrase/wikilink` | on | link `[text\|url]`
| `phrase/markdown` | on | link `[text](url)`
| `phrase/escaped-asterix` | on | asterisk escaped with a backslash
| `phrase/link` | on | phrase link `"text":url` or `**text**:url`; turning it off also removes the links `word:[url]`, `[text\|url]` and `[text](url)`
| `link/url` | on | URL in text as a link
| `link/email` | on | email in text as a link
| `link/definition` | on | link definition `[name]: url`
| `link/reference` | off | link written by name only `[name]`
| `image` | on | image `[* photo.jpg *]`
| `image/definition` | on | image definition `[*name*]: photo.jpg`
| `figure` | on | image with a caption
| `emoticon` | off | emoticons `:-)`
| `heading/underlined` | on | underlined headings
| `heading/surrounded` | on | headings `### Heading`
| `list` | on | lists
| `list/decimal`, `list/roman`, `list/alpha` | on | numbering `1.` and `1)`, `I.` and `I)`, `a)` and `A)`
| `list/definition` | on | definition lists
| `table` | on | tables
| `blockquote` | on | block quote `>`
| `horizline` | on | horizontal rule
| `blocks` | on | all `/--` blocks
| `block/code`, `block/pre`, `block/html`, `block/text`, `block/div`, `block/comment`, `block/default` | on | individual kinds of `/--` blocks
| `block/texysource` | off | the `/--texysource` block, which shows the HTML of its content as source code
| `fenced-code` | on | code block between <code>&#96;&#96;&#96;</code>
| `html/tag` | on | HTML tags in text
| `html/comment` | on | HTML comments
| `html/entity` | on | HTML entities such as `&amp;`
| `script` | on | directives `{{name: value}}`

Typography and hyphenation are not syntaxes; you turn them off in the [engine settings |#Typography].


Paragraphs
----------

```php
$texy->config->mergeLines = true;
```

By default, Texy joins the lines of a paragraph into one continuous text and starts a new line only where a line begins with a space. With `false`, every line break is kept.


Heading Balancing
-----------------

In Texy syntax, the author doesn't have to think about whether the article starts with a first-level or a second-level heading. Texy balances the headings: the most important heading in the article gets the `headingTop` level and the others line up below it, whichever underline the author started with. The underline character sets the importance: `#` is the most important, then `*`, `=` and `-`.

You can turn balancing off. Each underline character then has a fixed level, `#` the first, `*` the second, `=` the third and `-` the fourth, shifted by `headingTop`:

```php
$texy->config->headingBalancing = Texy\HeadingBalancing::Fixed;   // default Dynamic
```

For headings written with hashes at the start of the line, such as `### Heading`, `headingMoreMeansHigher` (default `true`) says that more hashes mean a more important heading.

Markdown does not balance headings. `#` is always the first level, `##` the second and so on, shifted only by `headingTop`.


Links Written by Name
---------------------

Authors don't have to type out the URLs that articles often link to. They write just a name in square brackets, and your function fills in the URL:

```php
$texy->config->linkResolver = fn(string $name) => match ($name) {
	'shop' => 'https://example.com/dryer',
	'wiki' => 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%s',  // %s stands for the link text
	default => null,
};
```

```texy
We bought our "dryer":[shop] cheaply and now use it to dry "plums":[wiki].
```

```html
<p>We bought our <a href="https://example.com/dryer">dryer</a> cheaply and now
use it to dry <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/plums">plums</a>.</p>
```

Texy asks the function once for each link whose name the article does not define itself. So a definition with the same name written directly in the article takes precedence. The function gets the name exactly as the author wrote it, including any anchor `#section`. It returns the URL, or `null` when it does not know the name, in which case the name itself is used as the URL. The returned URL is checked against the [allowed URLs |#Allowed URLs] like any other.

As its second parameter, the function gets the link node `Texy\Nodes\LinkNode` and may modify it, for example change its text in `$link->content`. You can tell a link written by name only, `[page]`, by `$link->isBareRef`. Images cannot be filled in this way: the author defines an image directly in the article, `[*logo*]: logo.png 100x50 .(Blog logo)`, or your [pass |custom-handlers#Modifying the Document] over `ImageNode` nodes sets its URL.

In Texy syntax, emphasized text can be a link too, such as `**recipe**:https://example.com`. If you don't want that, set `$texy->allowedSyntax['phrase/link'] = false` and no link is created from emphasis. Be careful: this turns off every link attached to text, including `"text":url`, `[text|url]`, `[text](url)` and `word:[url]`. Only URLs and email addresses written directly in the text remain.


Emoticons
---------

Emoticons are turned off by default. Once you turn them on, Texy replaces `:-)` with the 🙂 character and so on:

```php
$texy->allowedSyntax['emoticon'] = true;
$texy->config->emoticons[':-*'] = '😘';   // add your own
```

The default set is `:-)`, `:-(`, `;-)`, `:-D`, `8-O`, `8-)`, `:-?`, `:-x`, `:-P` and `:-|`. With `$texy->htmlOutput->emoticonClass` set, each emoticon is wrapped in a `<span>` with that class.


Lists
-----

Bulleted list items start with `*`, `-` or `+`, numbered ones with `1.`, `1)`, `I.`, `I)`, `a)` or `A)`. These characters are fixed, but you can turn off individual kinds of numbering as syntaxes:

```php
$texy->allowedSyntax['list/decimal'] = false;  // 1. and 1)
$texy->allowedSyntax['list/roman'] = false;    // I. and I)
$texy->allowedSyntax['list/alpha'] = false;    // a) and A)
```

A marker that is turned off stays plain text. The exceptions are `I)`, `V)` and `X)`, which are read as letters when Roman numerals are turned off, so lines `I) …` and `J) …` create a list labeled with letters starting at I.


Directives
----------

The syntax `{{name: value}}` in the text is a directive, handled by your code. The page [Customizing Elements |custom-handlers#Directives] shows how.

